---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import { celestial, scenes } from '../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
const crumbs = [{ label: 'Prepare for Your Reading', href: '/prepare-for-your-reading/' }];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Prepare for Your Reading"
  description="How to prepare for an astrology reading with Mo Lumen: confirm your birth time, think about the questions that matter, and know what to do if birth information is incomplete."
  path="/prepare-for-your-reading/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Before we meet</p>
        <h1>Prepare for your reading</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          You do not need to study astrology or arrive with a perfect list of questions. A little
          practical preparation simply gives us more room to spend the session on what matters.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.prepareReading.src}
        srcset={scenes.prepareReading.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.prepareReading.alt}
        width={scenes.prepareReading.width}
        height={scenes.prepareReading.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>1. Confirm your birth time if you can</h2>
      <p>
        Birth time gives us the ascendant and the chart's relationship to the local horizon and
        meridian, so it matters for houses and other time-sensitive techniques. In order of
        reliability:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Birth certificate</strong>: in the US, request the "long form" from the vital records office of your birth state; hospital-recorded times often appear there.</li>
        <li><strong>Baby book or hospital records</strong>: these can preserve a time recorded close to the birth.</li>
        <li><strong>Family memory</strong>: useful as a clue, but treat it as approximate. "Around dinnertime" is information; it is not the same as an exact time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If it isn't in the obvious place, the <a href="/birth-time/">birth time guide</a>
        walks through the search in order, including what to ask people who may remember and what
        to send me if all you have is a range.
      </p>
      <h2>2. If your birth time is unknown</h2>
      <p>
        Tell me rather than assuming you cannot have a useful reading. An unknown time limits the
        ascendant, houses, and some other techniques, but much of the chart remains available. We
        can work with what is solid and be explicit about what remains uncertain.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.clearedTable} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>3. Think about what is really on your mind</h2>
      <p>
        You do not need ten questions. One or two real ones are often more useful. "Why does every
        job start well and then begin to feel wrong?" gives us more to work with than "Tell me
        about my career." Specific questions help me understand where your attention already is;
        they do not lock the reading into a script.
      </p>
      <h2>4. Give yourself a comfortable place to talk</h2>
      <p>
        A quiet room, reliable audio, water, and perhaps a notebook are enough. You will receive a
        recording, so there is no need to capture every sentence while we are talking. If you can,
        leave yourself a little breathing room after the session rather than scheduling something
        demanding immediately afterward.
      </p>
      <h2>5. Come as you are</h2>
      <p>
        You do not need astrology vocabulary or a particular worldview to take part in the
        conversation. Bring your curiosity, your questions, your skepticism if you have some, and
        the life you are actually living. I will guide the astrology from there.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="That is enough preparation."
    body="Bring the birth information you actually have, what is on your mind, and yourself. I will take care of the astrology."
    buttonLabel="Review Reading FAQs"
    buttonHref="/frequently-asked-questions/"
    secondaryLabel="Still looking for your birth time?"
    secondaryHref="/birth-time/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
